const GNW_EMU_SM510_HTML = "<section class=\"gbemu-hero\"><div class=\"gbemu-hero-num\"><b>SM510</b><span>clean-room core</span></div><div class=\"gbemu-hero-txt\"><h3>How to build this chip, exactly as our Game &amp; Watch core runs it</h3><p>This tab is the complete build specification for the <b>SM510</b>: every register, every opcode with its timing and skips, reset, halt and wake, the divider, the display, the input, the sound, and every question no datasheet decides, answered the way this core answers it. The shared sections below it carry the implementation contract, the per-title data, save state, the corpus recipe and the acceptance tests a build must pass. Every claim carries its tier.</p></div></section>\n<nav class=\"gbemu-toc\"><b>On this tab</b> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510\">10: The Sharp SM510, as this core implements it</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-readme\">The Game &amp; Watch build specification: index, reading order, rules</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-00-machine\">The machine, whole: orientation before the part chapters</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-60-abi\">The implementation contract (ABI)</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-50-save-state\">The machine's save state: what must survive, and how the reference carries it</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-40-titles\">Per-title data: the 61 titles, their images, artwork, wiring, clock and demo</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-70-acceptance\">The acceptance tests a build must pass</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-80-corpus\">The corpus: how to obtain the images, and how to know you have the right ones</a></nav>\n<section class=\"sect gwemu-chapter\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510\">10: The Sharp SM510, as this core implements it</h3><nav class=\"gbemu-toc\"><b>Sections</b> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s1\">1. Scope</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s2\">2. Registers and memory</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s3\">3. Program counter and program memory layout</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s4\">4. Instruction set</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s5\">5. Execution model</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s6\">6. Reset (ACL) state</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s7\">7. Standby, halt and wake</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s8\">8. Divider and the one-second flag</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s9\">9. Display</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s10\">10. Input</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s11\">11. Sound</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s12\">12. Registered choices</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s13\">13. Constants</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s14\">14. Self-check vectors</a> <a href=\"#gwemu-10-sm510-s15\">15. Where this chapter's facts came from</a></nav>\n<p><em>Written 2026-10-03. Supersedes every earlier description of this part.</em></p>\n<p>The SM510 is the 4-bit CMOS microcontroller inside 18 of the Game &amp; Watch titles in the corpus. This chapter is a complete build specification of the part <b>as the scored ABI presents it</b>: every register and its width, the 128-nibble RAM with its two display files, the paged program memory addressed by a 63-state polynomial counter, all 256 first-byte encodings, the instruction slot and what it costs in oscillator ticks, the two reset vectors, the CEND halt and both wake sources, the 15-stage divider and the gamma flag, the LCD plane, the key matrix with its K flip-flop, and the R-pin buzzer level. Where no datasheet page decides a behaviour, this chapter states <b>the core's default</b>, and you must match it. A builder who follows this text exactly reproduces the core's per-instruction trajectory: PC, RAM, LCD plane, R pins and halt. Rival readings are named so that you can recognise them. They are <b>NOT SCORED</b>.</p>\n<p>Citation keys: <code>[90]</code> is the Sharp 1990 Microcomputers Data Book and <code>[86]</code> is the Sharp 1986 MOS Semiconductor Data Book. The tiers are those of <code>README.md</code> §3.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s1\">1. Scope</h4>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>Part:</b> SM510. The SM511/SM512 and the SM5A have their own chapters. Some facts here are shared with them, and every such case is stated explicitly. Nothing is carried across a part boundary silently.</li><li><b>What is scored:</b> the machine as <code>adapter/site-abi.mjs</code> constructs it. That is the \"site surface\", the eighteen members the site's <code>GnwEmulator</code> touches (§13 lists them). The adapter builds the core with these settings:\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><code>wakeRelatch: \"strobe\"</code> (§7.3; until 28 Oct 2026 the adapter chose \"external\").</li><li>beta and BA held at their pull-up level. The site's <code>inB</code>/<code>inBA</code> writes are stored but are <b>not</b> forwarded, unless <code>coreCfg.sitePins</code> is set, which is NOT SCORED.</li><li>Every other option at the core default.</li><li><code>step()</code> wraps the core's instruction slot so that <b>a waking call also executes the first instruction at the wake vector</b> (§7.4).</li></ul></li><li><b>Out of scope:</b> the per-title segment-to-artwork map, the key wiring tables (<code>inRows</code> arrive already in strobe order; see the wiring chapter), the speaker/resampler path and the LCD optical model. Their registry rows are still listed in §12, because §12 must cover every key.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s2\">2. Registers and memory</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">2.1 Register file</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Register</th><th>Width</th><th>Written by</th><th>Read by</th><th>ACL value</th><th>Tier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>P_U</code></td><td>2 bits</td><td>jumps, calls, returns, ACL, wake</td><td>fetch</td><td>3</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p90 widths; value [90] p154 (7)</td></tr><tr><td><code>P_M</code></td><td>4 bits</td><td>jumps, calls, returns, ACL, wake</td><td>fetch</td><td>7</td><td>ADJUDICATED as above</td></tr><tr><td><code>P_L</code></td><td>6 bits, a polynomial counter (§3)</td><td>every instruction</td><td>fetch</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p154 bullet 2; value [90] p154 (7)</td></tr><tr><td>Stack <code>S</code> (<code>S_U S_M S_L</code>)</td><td>2+4+6 = 12 bits</td><td><code>TM</code>, <code>TML</code> push; <code>RTN0/1</code> pop</td><td>pop</td><td>0:0:0</td><td>widths ADJUDICATED [86] p90; ACL value MECHANISM (the core zeroes everything it constructs)</td></tr><tr><td>Stack <code>R</code> (<code>R_U R_M R_L</code>)</td><td>12 bits</td><td>push (takes old S)</td><td>pop (moves into S)</td><td>0:0:0</td><td>as above</td></tr><tr><td><code>Acc</code></td><td>4 bits</td><td>many</td><td>many</td><td>0</td><td>width ADJUDICATED [86] p90; ACL value MECHANISM (zeroed)</td></tr><tr><td><code>C</code> (carry)</td><td>1 bit</td><td>only <code>ADD11</code>, <code>ROT</code>, <code>RC</code>, <code>SC</code></td><td><code>ADD11</code>, <code>ROT</code>, <code>TC</code>, <code>BDC</code></td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157 (only these rows name C)</td></tr><tr><td><code>B_M</code> (RAM file)</td><td><b>3 bits</b></td><td><code>LB</code>, <code>LBL</code>, <code>EXC*</code>/<code>LDA</code> XOR</td><td>RAM addressing</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p90 <code>B_M(3)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>B_L</code> (RAM word)</td><td>4 bits</td><td><code>LB</code>, <code>LBL</code>, <code>EXBLA</code>, <code>INCB</code>, <code>DECB</code>, <code>EXCI</code>, <code>EXCD</code></td><td>RAM addressing, <code>TABL</code>, <code>EXBLA</code></td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p90 <code>B_L(4)</code></td></tr><tr><td>SBM one-shot</td><td>1 flag</td><td><code>SBM</code></td><td>the next instruction's RAM addressing</td><td>clear</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157 \"only next step\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>W</code></td><td>8 bits, a shift register</td><td><code>WR</code>, <code>WS</code></td><td>strobe pins S1–S8, key matrix</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p90/p91; there is <b>no</b> parallel load (<code>PTW</code> on [90] p152 (3) is erratum E6)</td></tr><tr><td><code>L</code></td><td>4 bits</td><td><code>ATL</code></td><td>the <code>bs</code> segment pin (§9)</td><td>0</td><td>width ADJUDICATED [86] p90; role NOT ESTABLISHED (<code>BS_AND_L</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>Y</code></td><td>4 bits</td><td><code>ATFC</code></td><td><b>nothing</b></td><td>0</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED (<code>ATFC_DESTINATION</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>R</code> (buzzer latch)</td><td>2 bits</td><td><code>ATR</code></td><td>R-pin level (§11)</td><td>0</td><td>width ADJUDICATED [86] p90 <code>R(2)</code>; bit choice NOT ESTABLISHED</td></tr><tr><td><code>BP</code></td><td>4 bits stored, only bit 0 used</td><td><code>ATBP</code></td><td>LCD common gate (§9)</td><td><b>1</b></td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED (<code>BP_TO_COMMONS</code>); see §6</td></tr><tr><td><code>BC</code></td><td>1 bit</td><td><code>BDC</code> (BC ← C)</td><td>LCD blanking (§9)</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED polarity [90] p157, [90] p152 (5)</td></tr><tr><td>K flip-flop</td><td>4 bits (output <code>kLatch</code>, input <code>kPending</code>)</td><td>clocked at each running slot; input re-sampled on events (§10)</td><td><code>KTA</code></td><td>see §6</td><td>ADJUDICATED existence [86] p90 / [90] p150 \"K : Key input F/F\"</td></tr><tr><td>wake line</td><td>1 flag</td><td><code>updateKLine()</code> (site), reset</td><td>halted-slot wake test</td><td>see §6</td><td>MECHANISM [90] p154 (5)</td></tr><tr><td>gamma</td><td>1 bit</td><td>set by the divider edge; cleared by <code>TIS</code></td><td><code>TIS</code>, halted-slot wake test</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p154 (4)</td></tr><tr><td><code>DIV</code> (divider count)</td><td>15 bits</td><td>every oscillator tick; <code>IDIV</code> clears it</td><td><code>TF1</code>, <code>TF4</code>, gamma edge, R pins</td><td>0</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p90 <code>DIV(15)</code>, [90] p154 (4)</td></tr><tr><td>skip latch</td><td>1 flag</td><td>any satisfied skip condition</td><td>the next slot</td><td>clear</td><td>MECHANISM (<code>SKIP_COSTS_A_CYCLE</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>LAX-succession flag</td><td>1 flag</td><td>every executed instruction; cleared by a skip slot</td><td><code>LAX</code></td><td>clear</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED (<code>LAX_SUCCESSION</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>halted</td><td>1 flag</td><td><code>CEND</code> sets it; a wake clears it</td><td><code>step()</code></td><td>clear</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p154 (5)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>beta and BA are input <b>pins</b>, not registers. At the ABI they always read <b>1</b> (§10).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">2.2 RAM map</h5>\n<p>The RAM holds <b>128 nibbles = 8 files × 16 words × 4 bits = 512 bits</b> [ADJUDICATED [90] p154 (3), Fig. 3; [86] p90 <code>RAM (96x4)</code> + <code>Display RAM (32x4)</code>]. The cell index is <b><code>B_M × 16 + B_L</code></b>. While the SBM one-shot is armed, the effective <code>B_M</code> is <code>B_M OR 4</code> for that one instruction (§4, <code>SBM</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>B_M</th><th>File name</th><th>Index range</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>X</td><td>0x00-0x0F</td><td>general</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Y</td><td>0x10-0x1F</td><td>general</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Z</td><td>0x20-0x2F</td><td>general</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>M</td><td>0x30-0x3F</td><td>general</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>P</td><td>0x40-0x4F</td><td>general</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Q</td><td>0x50-0x5F</td><td>general</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><b>R</b></td><td><b>0x60-0x6F</b></td><td><b>display file, segment group <code>a</code> (a1–a16)</b></td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><b>S</b></td><td><b>0x70-0x7F</b></td><td><b>display file, segment group <code>b</code> (b1–b16)</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>[ADJUDICATED [90] p154 Fig. 3 (file names and codes, the thick frame round R and S)]</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>The display RAM is part of the one RAM, not a separate buffer. Any write to files 6 and 7 is a write to the screen. This includes <code>EXC</code>, <code>EXCI</code>, <code>EXCD</code>, <code>SM</code> and <code>RM</code> [MECHANISM].</li><li>The ABI's <code>ram</code> member <b>is</b> this 128-entry array, one nibble per byte, values 0–15. A write through the ABI lands in the core directly [MECHANISM].</li><li>At ACL every cell is 0 [MECHANISM: the core clears all RAM on power-on. NOT PRINTED for the silicon, [90] p149–p158 checked].</li><li><code>[90] p149</code> prints \"94×4\" for the data RAM. That is erratum E4; the correct figure is 96×4.</li></ul>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s3\">3. Program counter and program memory layout</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">3.1 Addressing</h5>\n<p>A program address is <b><code>P_U × 1024 + P_M × 64 + P_L</code></b>, giving 12 bits and a 4096-byte image index. <code>P_L</code> is the counter's <b>raw 6-bit state</b>, not its position in the visiting order [ADJUDICATED widths [86] p90; MECHANISM index]. The ABI's <code>pc</code> member returns exactly this flattened value.</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>A page</b> is one (<code>P_U</code>, <code>P_M</code>) pair and holds <b>63 steps</b> [ADJUDICATED [90] p164 Fig. 1 \"1 page consists of 63 steps\"].</li><li><b>The program memory</b> is 2,772 bytes = <b>44 pages × 63</b> [ADJUDICATED [86] p90 (2,772 x 8); [90] p153 (2) prints \"2,772-bit\", which is erratum E2].</li><li><b>Reachable pages.</b> <code>P_M</code> 0–10 can be reached in each of the four <code>P_U</code> quadrants. <code>TL</code> stops at <code>0x7A</code>, so <code>P_M</code> 11–15 is unreachable by any jump [MECHANISM]. Corpus images are 4096-byte files in which <code>P_M</code> &gt; 10 is filler, and index 0x3F of each page is never fetched.</li><li><b>Fetch past the image.</b> If the image is shorter than 4096 bytes, a fetch beyond its end reads 0x00 (<code>SKIP</code>) [MECHANISM]. This cannot happen with the corpus.</li></ul>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">3.2 The polynomial <code>P_L</code> counter</h5>\n<p>\"For the instructions except for a jump instruction, the polynomial counter P_L is shifted by 1 step according to a polynomial code\" [ADJUDICATED [90] p154 bullet 2]. The page prints <b>no</b> polynomial (UNPRINTED-CLAIM: SUBJECT polynomial ; taps|feedback|code.).</p>\n<p><b>The rule this core uses</b> is <code>right/taps=0b000011/XNOR</code>, the pc.mjs <code>DEFAULT_RULE</code>. Let <code>b0</code> and <code>b1</code> be bits 0 and 1 of the current state. The next state is computed in two steps:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Shift the 6-bit state <b>right</b> by one.</li><li>Fill bit 5 with <b>NOT (b0 XOR b1)</b>.</li></ol>\n<p>[INSTRUMENT: <code>tools/pl_program_measure.mjs</code> §2e. A page of real code must be <b>tileable</b> by instructions of the right widths: the widths must close exactly on 63 steps and never land an opcode on an unlisted encoding. Over 773 programmed pages of 18 SM510 images, this rule leaves 0 pages untileable. The next best of the 12 structurally valid shift rules leaves 43 and the worst leaves 88. Denominator: 12 rules, 18 images, 773 pages, 1 consistent. Registry <code>P_L_SEQUENCE</code>.]</p>\n<p><b>Structure</b> [ADJUDICATED]:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>The counter visits one <b>63-cycle</b>.</li><li><b>0x3F is the lock state</b>: its successor is itself [[90] p124, SM500's inhibit code P_L = 111111].</li><li>From reset (<code>P_L</code> = 0) the counter returns to 0 after exactly 63 steps.</li><li><code>succ(s) = s + 1</code> holds for <b>0 of the 63</b> states, so \"PC + 1\" is never arithmetic.</li></ul>\n<p><b>The full 63-state sequence from <code>P_L</code> = 0.</b> Position <em>n</em> is the state after <em>n</em> steps. This order is also the order in which a straight-line program occupies a page.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>n</th><th>P_L</th><th>n</th><th>P_L</th><th>n</th><th>P_L</th><th>n</th><th>P_L</th><th>n</th><th>P_L</th><th>n</th><th>P_L</th><th>n</th><th>P_L</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0x00</td><td>9</td><td>0x3B</td><td>18</td><td>0x17</td><td>27</td><td>0x28</td><td>36</td><td>0x2C</td><td>45</td><td>0x04</td><td>54</td><td>0x13</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0x20</td><td>10</td><td>0x3D</td><td>19</td><td>0x2B</td><td>28</td><td>0x34</td><td>37</td><td>0x36</td><td>46</td><td>0x22</td><td>55</td><td>0x29</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0x30</td><td>11</td><td>0x1E</td><td>20</td><td>0x35</td><td>29</td><td>0x3A</td><td>38</td><td>0x1B</td><td>47</td><td>0x11</td><td>56</td><td>0x14</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0x38</td><td>12</td><td>0x0F</td><td>21</td><td>0x1A</td><td>30</td><td>0x1D</td><td>39</td><td>0x2D</td><td>48</td><td>0x08</td><td>57</td><td>0x2A</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0x3C</td><td>13</td><td>0x27</td><td>22</td><td>0x0D</td><td>31</td><td>0x0E</td><td>40</td><td>0x16</td><td>49</td><td>0x24</td><td>58</td><td>0x15</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0x3E</td><td>14</td><td>0x33</td><td>23</td><td>0x06</td><td>32</td><td>0x07</td><td>41</td><td>0x0B</td><td>50</td><td>0x32</td><td>59</td><td>0x0A</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0x1F</td><td>15</td><td>0x39</td><td>24</td><td>0x03</td><td>33</td><td>0x23</td><td>42</td><td>0x25</td><td>51</td><td>0x19</td><td>60</td><td>0x05</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>0x2F</td><td>16</td><td>0x1C</td><td>25</td><td>0x21</td><td>34</td><td>0x31</td><td>43</td><td>0x12</td><td>52</td><td>0x0C</td><td>61</td><td>0x02</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>0x37</td><td>17</td><td>0x2E</td><td>26</td><td>0x10</td><td>35</td><td>0x18</td><td>44</td><td>0x09</td><td>53</td><td>0x26</td><td>62</td><td>0x01</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>After n = 62 (0x01) comes 0x00 again. The successor of 0x3F is 0x3F.</p>\n<p><b>Successor table</b> (state → next), for direct lookup:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>s</th><th>+0</th><th>+1</th><th>+2</th><th>+3</th><th>+4</th><th>+5</th><th>+6</th><th>+7</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>00h</td><td>20</td><td>00</td><td>01</td><td>21</td><td>22</td><td>02</td><td>03</td><td>23</td></tr><tr><td>08h</td><td>24</td><td>04</td><td>05</td><td>25</td><td>26</td><td>06</td><td>07</td><td>27</td></tr><tr><td>10h</td><td>28</td><td>08</td><td>09</td><td>29</td><td>2A</td><td>0A</td><td>0B</td><td>2B</td></tr><tr><td>18h</td><td>2C</td><td>0C</td><td>0D</td><td>2D</td><td>2E</td><td>0E</td><td>0F</td><td>2F</td></tr><tr><td>20h</td><td>30</td><td>10</td><td>11</td><td>31</td><td>32</td><td>12</td><td>13</td><td>33</td></tr><tr><td>28h</td><td>34</td><td>14</td><td>15</td><td>35</td><td>36</td><td>16</td><td>17</td><td>37</td></tr><tr><td>30h</td><td>38</td><td>18</td><td>19</td><td>39</td><td>3A</td><td>1A</td><td>1B</td><td>3B</td></tr><tr><td>38h</td><td>3C</td><td>1C</td><td>1D</td><td>3D</td><td>3E</td><td>1E</td><td>1F</td><td><b>3F</b></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>All values are hexadecimal. Row 0x38, column +7 is the lock state 0x3F.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">3.3 Page wrap</h5>\n<p><b>There is none.</b> <code>P_U</code> and <code>P_M</code> are never changed by the counter's step, and stepping 63 times returns to the starting state. Straight-line code therefore cycles inside its page forever, and code leaves a page only by a jump, call, return, reset or wake [MECHANISM, <code>PAGE_WRAP</code>].</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">3.4 The lock state</h5>\n<p><code>P_L</code> can be loaded with 0x3F by any of these:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><code>T 0xBF</code>;</li><li>a <code>TL</code> or <code>TML</code> operand whose low six bits are 0x3F;</li><li>an IDX word whose low six bits are 0x3F;</li><li><code>ATPL</code> with <code>P_L5</code> = <code>P_L4</code> = 1 and <code>Acc</code> = 0xF;</li><li>a return to a pushed 0x3F.</li></ul>\n<p>When the CPU is at <code>P_L</code> = 0x3F, every running slot does the following [MECHANISM]:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>clocks the K flip-flop;</li><li>advances the oscillator 2 ticks;</li><li>executes <b>nothing</b>, so PC, RAM and registers are unchanged;</li><li>leaves a set skip latch <b>set</b>: the lock test comes before the skip latch (§5.1 step 3). This matters only for a return by <code>RTN1</code> to a pushed 0x3F, the one way to arrive with the latch set.</li></ul>\n<p><code>halt</code> stays 0, the divider and gamma keep running, and nothing ever leaves this state except a reset. Nothing in the corpus reaches it.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">3.5 The subroutine cover page, IDX and TM</h5>\n<p>Page <code>P_U</code> = 0, <code>P_M</code> = 0 is the <b>subroutine cover page</b> [ADJUDICATED [90] p153 Fig. 1 label]. The core treats it as a <b>table of one-byte IDX words, not as code</b> [MECHANISM, <code>COVER_PAGE_IS_IDX</code>, <code>IDX_STRUCTURE</code>].</p>\n<p><code>TM</code> (0xC0-0xFE) is a <b>one-byte, one-slot</b> call [<code>TM_BYTE_COUNT</code>]. In a single slot it does three things:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>Push.</b> It pushes the return site: the <b>same page</b>, <code>P_L</code> advanced <b>one</b> polynomial step past the <code>TM</code>.</li><li><b>Read the IDX word.</b> It reads the program byte at cover-page step <code>P_L</code> = (opcode AND 0x3F), which is program index 0x000–0x03E.</li><li><b>Jump</b> to the address the word encodes:\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><code>P_U</code> ← word bits 7–6;</li><li><code>P_M</code> ← <b>4</b> (binary 0100);</li><li><code>P_L</code> ← word bits 5–0.</li></ul></li></ol>\n<p><b>Why the IDX word is one byte</b> [MECHANISM]. The merged TM/IDX row on [90] p157 has the same letter layout as TML's row, and on that page a letter names a bit field, not a byte. In addition, 63 one-byte entries fit a 63-step page exactly, and two-byte entries would not fit.</p>\n<p><b>Reaching the page without TM.</b> If the PC reaches page (0,0) by any other route (for example a <code>TL</code> with <code>P_U</code> = <code>P_M</code> = 0, or a return to a zero stack), the core records an internal event and <b>executes the byte as ordinary code</b>. Nothing else changes, and nothing at the ABI marks it [MECHANISM].</p>\n<p><b>Rival (NOT SCORED):</b> <code>coverPageIsIdx: false</code>, in which <code>TM</code> jumps into page (0,0) and executes it as code.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s4\">4. Instruction set</h4>\n<p>Every one of the 256 first bytes is listed below:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>48 mnemonics</b>, covering 252 encodings;</li><li><b>one alias</b>, <code>DC</code> = 0x3A, inside <code>ADX</code>;</li><li><b>four unlisted encodings</b>: 0x50, 0x5C, 0x7B, 0xFF.</li></ul>\n<p>[ADJUDICATED [90] p157. The partition control in <code>spec/isa_sm510.mjs</code> proves claimed + unlisted = 256 with zero overlaps.]</p>\n<p><b>Conventions</b></p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><code>M</code> is the RAM nibble at the effective (<code>B_M</code>, <code>B_L</code>).</li><li>\"next\" means the address after this whole instruction: one polynomial step for 1-byte instructions, two for 2-byte instructions.</li><li><b>Time:</b> every row costs <b>1 slot = 2 oscillator ticks</b>, whatever its width (§5.4).</li><li><b>Skip:</b> \"skips\" means the <em>next whole instruction</em> is retired as a no-op in the following slot (§5.2).</li><li>Bits are written I7..I0 for the opcode and O7..O0 for an operand byte.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Encoding</th><th>Mnemonic</th><th>Width</th><th>Operation (exact)</th><th>Skip behaviour</th><th>Time</th><th>Tier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0x00</td><td><code>SKIP</code></td><td>1</td><td>No operation.</td><td>none</td><td>1 slot / 2 ticks</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157</td></tr><tr><td>0x01</td><td><code>ATBP</code></td><td>1</td><td>BP ← Acc (all 4 bits stored; only bit 0 is ever consulted, §9).</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED transfer; gating NOT ESTABLISHED (<code>BP_TO_COMMONS</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>0x02</td><td><code>SBM</code></td><td>1</td><td>Arms a one-shot: the <b>next instruction that executes</b> addresses RAM with B_M OR 4. B_M itself is unchanged. The arm survives a skip slot and an unlisted byte, and is consumed by the next instruction that decodes as a listed mnemonic, whether or not that instruction touches RAM.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157 \"B_M2 ← 1 (only next step)\"; carry-through MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td>0x03</td><td><code>ATPL</code></td><td>1</td><td>P_L ← (P_L AND 0x30) OR Acc, where P_L is ATPL's own step. P_U and P_M are unchanged. This is a <b>jump</b>: no polynomial step follows, and the next instruction executes at exactly the value written.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p154 bullets 2–3 (<code>ATPL_ORDER</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>0x04-0x07</td><td><code>RM</code></td><td>1</td><td>M bit <em>i</em> ← 0, <em>i</em> = I1..I0.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157</td></tr><tr><td>0x08</td><td><code>ADD</code></td><td>1</td><td>Acc ← (Acc + M) AND 0xF. C is <b>not</b> read and <b>not</b> written.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157</td></tr><tr><td>0x09</td><td><code>ADD11</code></td><td>1</td><td>Let <em>sum</em> = Acc + M + C. Then C ← 1 if <em>sum</em> &gt; 15, else 0; Acc ← <em>sum</em> AND 0xF.</td><td>skips if the new C = 1</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157</td></tr><tr><td>0x0A</td><td><code>COMA</code></td><td>1</td><td>Acc ← NOT Acc (4 bits).</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x0B</td><td><code>EXBLA</code></td><td>1</td><td>Acc ↔ B_L.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x0C-0x0F</td><td><code>SM</code></td><td>1</td><td>M bit <em>i</em> ← 1, <em>i</em> = I1..I0.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x10-0x13</td><td><code>EXC</code></td><td>1</td><td>First Acc ↔ M (at the effective B_M). Then B_M ← B_M XOR (I1..I0). The XOR touches only bits 1–0, so B_M2 is unaffected.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157</td></tr><tr><td>0x14-0x17</td><td><code>EXCI</code></td><td>1</td><td>As <code>EXC</code>. Then <b>test</b> B_L = 0xF on the <b>old</b> value, then B_L ← (B_L + 1) AND 0xF.</td><td>skips if the old B_L was 0xF</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x18-0x1B</td><td><code>LDA</code></td><td>1</td><td>Acc ← M. Then B_M ← B_M XOR (I1..I0). RAM is not written.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x1C-0x1F</td><td><code>EXCD</code></td><td>1</td><td>As <code>EXC</code>. Then <b>test</b> B_L = 0 on the <b>old</b> value, then B_L ← (B_L − 1) AND 0xF.</td><td>skips if the old B_L was 0</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x20-0x2F</td><td><code>LAX</code></td><td>1</td><td>Acc ← I3..I0, <b>unless the previous executed instruction was a LAX</b> (§5.3). A suppressed LAX changes nothing but still costs its slot.</td><td>(suppression is not a skip and arms no skip latch)</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>operand bits ADJUDICATED via [90] p126 (p157's \"I4–I1\" is erratum E8); succession NOT ESTABLISHED (<code>LAX_SUCCESSION</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>0x30-0x39</td><td><code>ADX</code></td><td>1</td><td>Let <em>sum</em> = Acc + (I3..I0). Acc ← <em>sum</em> AND 0xF. <b>C is not written.</b></td><td>skips if <em>sum</em> &gt; 15</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157</td></tr><tr><td>0x3A</td><td><code>DC</code></td><td>1</td><td><code>ADX</code> with immediate 10: Acc ← (Acc + 10) AND 0xF. C is not written.</td><td><b>never skips</b></td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p126 \"No skip if I3I2I1I0 = 1010\" (<code>DC_SKIP_BEHAVIOUR</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>0x3B-0x3F</td><td><code>ADX</code></td><td>1</td><td>As 0x30-0x39.</td><td>skips if <em>sum</em> &gt; 15</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x40-0x4F</td><td><code>LB</code></td><td>1</td><td><div>Let <em>h</em> = I3 OR I2. Then:</div><ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>B_L3 ← <em>h</em>; B_L2 ← <em>h</em>; B_L1 ← I3; B_L0 ← I2.</li><li>B_M1..B_M0 ← I1..I0.</li><li><b>B_M2 is preserved.</b></li></ul><div>So B_L = 0x0 for 0x40-0x43, 0xD for 0x44-0x47, 0xE for 0x48-0x4B, and 0xF for 0x4C-0x4F.</div></td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>B_L: INSTRUMENT (<code>LB_HIGH_NIBBLE</code>); B_M2: MECHANISM (<code>LB_BM2</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>0x50</td><td><em>(unlisted)</em></td><td>none</td><td>See §4.1.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED unlisted [90] p157; ABI behaviour MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td>0x51</td><td><code>TB</code></td><td>1</td><td>Test the beta pin. At the ABI beta is always 1.</td><td>skips if beta = 1, so <b>at the ABI it always skips</b></td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED pull-up [86] p91; held at the ABI: CONVERGENCE</td></tr><tr><td>0x52</td><td><code>TC</code></td><td>1</td><td>Test carry.</td><td>skips if C = <b>0</b></td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157</td></tr><tr><td>0x53</td><td><code>TAM</code></td><td>1</td><td>Compare Acc with M.</td><td>skips if Acc = M</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x54-0x57</td><td><code>TMI</code></td><td>1</td><td>Test M bit <em>i</em>, <em>i</em> = I1..I0.</td><td>skips if that bit = 1</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x58</td><td><code>TIS</code></td><td>1</td><td>Test gamma, then gamma ← 0 <b>unconditionally</b>.</td><td>skips if gamma was 0</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157 \"Skip if 1S = 0, γ ← 0\"; [90] p127 SM500 <code>TG</code></td></tr><tr><td>0x59</td><td><code>ATL</code></td><td>1</td><td>L ← Acc.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x5A</td><td><code>TA0</code></td><td>1</td><td>Test Acc.</td><td>skips if Acc = 0</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x5B</td><td><code>TABL</code></td><td>1</td><td>Compare Acc with B_L.</td><td>skips if Acc = B_L</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x5C</td><td><em>(unlisted)</em></td><td>none</td><td>See §4.1.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>as 0x50</td></tr><tr><td>0x5D</td><td><code>CEND</code></td><td>1</td><td>Sets halted. The next PC (CEND + 1 step) is stored as usual but never executes, because the wake goes to 1:0:0 (§7).</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157, p154 (5)</td></tr><tr><td>0x5E</td><td><code>TAL</code></td><td>1</td><td>Test the BA pin. At the ABI BA is always 1.</td><td>skips if BA = 1, so <b>at the ABI it always skips</b></td><td>1 / 2</td><td>as <code>TB</code></td></tr><tr><td>0x5F+0x00-0xFF</td><td><code>LBL</code></td><td>2</td><td>B_M ← O6..O4; B_L ← O3..O0. Operand bit 7 is ignored.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157; O7 ignored MECHANISM (B_M is 3 bits)</td></tr><tr><td>0x60</td><td><code>ATFC</code></td><td>1</td><td>Y ← Acc. Y drives nothing.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>transfer ADJUDICATED; destination NOT ESTABLISHED (<code>ATFC_DESTINATION</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>0x61</td><td><code>ATR</code></td><td>1</td><td>R ← Acc AND 3 (R1 = Acc0, R2 = Acc1).</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED (<code>ATR_WHICH_BITS</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>0x62</td><td><code>WR</code></td><td>1</td><td>W ← (W × 2) AND 0xFF, shifting in 0 at bit 0.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x63</td><td><code>WS</code></td><td>1</td><td>W ← (W × 2 + 1) AND 0xFF, shifting in 1 at bit 0.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x64</td><td><code>INCB</code></td><td>1</td><td>Test B_L = 0xF on the old value, then B_L ← (B_L + 1) AND 0xF.</td><td>skips if the old B_L was 0xF</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x65</td><td><code>IDIV</code></td><td>1</td><td>Divider count ← 0, <b>all 15 stages</b>. gamma is untouched.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157 \"DIV ← 0\" (all-stage scope MECHANISM; SM500 prints f9–f1 only, a different part)</td></tr><tr><td>0x66</td><td><code>RC</code></td><td>1</td><td>C ← 0.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x67</td><td><code>SC</code></td><td>1</td><td>C ← 1.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x68</td><td><code>TF1</code></td><td>1</td><td>Read divider bit <b>14</b> (1 Hz).</td><td>skips if that bit = 1</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED (<code>DIVIDER_TAPS_TF1_TF4</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>0x69</td><td><code>TF4</code></td><td>1</td><td>Read divider bit <b>11</b> (8 Hz).</td><td>skips if that bit = 1</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED (same row)</td></tr><tr><td>0x6A</td><td><code>KTA</code></td><td>1</td><td>Acc ← K flip-flop output (§10.2).</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157</td></tr><tr><td>0x6B</td><td><code>ROT</code></td><td>1</td><td>Rotate right through carry, 5 bits round: new C ← old Acc0; Acc ← (old Acc shifted right one bit) with old C entering bit 3.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157 loop diagram</td></tr><tr><td>0x6C</td><td><code>DECB</code></td><td>1</td><td>Test B_L = 0 on the old value, then B_L ← (B_L − 1) AND 0xF.</td><td>skips if the old B_L was 0</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td>0x6D</td><td><code>BDC</code></td><td>1</td><td>BC ← C. <b>C = 1 turns the display off.</b></td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157</td></tr><tr><td>0x6E</td><td><code>RTN0</code></td><td>1</td><td>Pop: P ← S; S ← R; R unchanged.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157 chain</td></tr><tr><td>0x6F</td><td><code>RTN1</code></td><td>1</td><td>As <code>RTN0</code>, then arm a skip. The instruction at the <b>returned-to</b> address is skipped.</td><td>always skips</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157</td></tr><tr><td>0x70-0x7A+0x00-0xFF</td><td><code>TL</code></td><td>2</td><td>P_M ← I3..I0 (0–10); P_U ← O7..O6; P_L ← O5..O0.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157 (operand 0xFF, printed out of range, is executed by the same rule and lands on the lock)</td></tr><tr><td>0x7B</td><td><em>(unlisted)</em></td><td>none</td><td>See §4.1.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>as 0x50</td></tr><tr><td>0x7C-0x7F+0x00-0xFF</td><td><code>TML</code></td><td>2</td><td>First push the step <b>two</b> polynomial steps past the TML, in the same page. Then P_M ← I1..I0 (P_M3, P_M2 ← 0); P_U ← O7..O6; P_L ← O5..O0.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157; return site MECHANISM (<code>RETURN_ADDRESS_SPANS_OPERAND</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>0x80-0xBF</td><td><code>T</code></td><td>1</td><td>P_L ← I5..I0; P_U and P_M unchanged.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157</td></tr><tr><td>0xC0-0xFE</td><td><code>TM</code></td><td>1</td><td>Push the step <b>one</b> polynomial step past the TM. Read the IDX word at program index (I5..I0). Then P_U ← word bits 7–6; P_M ← 4; P_L ← word bits 5–0 (§3.5).</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>MECHANISM (<code>TM_BYTE_COUNT</code>, <code>IDX_STRUCTURE</code>, <code>COVER_PAGE_IS_IDX</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>0xFF</td><td><em>(unlisted)</em></td><td>none</td><td>See §4.1.</td><td>none</td><td>1 / 2</td><td>as 0x50</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">4.1 Unlisted encodings at the ABI</h5>\n<p>0x50, 0x5C, 0x7B and 0xFF are not instructions [ADJUDICATED [90] p157 lists nothing at them]. The ABI runs with <code>stopOnUnlisted: false</code>, so meeting one has these effects [MECHANISM]:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>P_L takes <b>one</b> polynomial step.</li><li>One slot (2 ticks) elapses, after the K flip-flop has clocked.</li><li><b>Nothing else changes.</b> Acc, C, RAM, B, W, L, Y, R, BP and BC are unchanged, and no skip is armed.</li><li>The SBM one-shot, if armed, <b>stays armed</b> for the next listed instruction.</li><li>The LAX-succession flag is <b>not updated</b>. The previous executed instruction is still the one that counts, so for example LAX, 0x50, LAX suppresses the second LAX.</li><li><code>step()</code> returns a result whose mnemonic is <code>\"??\"</code>. Neither <code>_twCount</code> nor <code>_cendCount</code> moves.</li><li>The core records an internal \"unlisted-encoding\" event. This is not observable at the ABI.</li></ol>\n<p>If a <b>skip</b> lands on an unlisted byte, its width is taken as 1 (§5.2).</p>\n<p><b>Rival (NOT SCORED):</b> <code>stopOnUnlisted: true</code>, which stops the machine instead.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s5\">5. Execution model</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">5.1 One slot</h5>\n<p>The core's unit of time is the <b>instruction slot</b>. It is <b>2 oscillator ticks</b> [ADJUDICATED [90] p149 F6 \"Instruction cycle: 61 µs (TYP.)\" = 1/16,384 Hz; system clock = 32,768 Hz ÷ 2, where [90] p154 (6) prints \"one second\" as erratum E3 for \"one half\"]. The ABI's <code>clkDiv</code> returns <b>2</b>.</p>\n<p>A <b>running</b> slot performs exactly these steps, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>Clock the K flip-flop:</b> kLatch ← kPending (§10.2).</li><li><b>Advance the oscillator 2 ticks.</b> Each tick increments the 15-bit divider count modulo 32768, and sets gamma if the count wrapped to 0 (§8). [CONVERGENCE. Reason: the reference port's step advances its divider before it executes. With the start-of-cycle reading, <code>TF4</code> two periods early was the first lockstep divergence on 8 SM510 images. Board §24.1.]</li><li><b>If P_L = 0x3F, execute nothing</b> (§3.4): <code>step()</code> returns the stopped result and the slot ends. This test comes <b>before</b> the skip latch is looked at, so a skip latch that is set stays set. [MECHANISM: <code>src/cpu.mjs</code> <code>step()</code> tests the lock first. The order is NOT ESTABLISHED: no corpus image reaches 0x3F, so no run tells the two orders apart. It is the same order as SM511/512 (30-SM511-SM512.md §5).]</li><li><b>Execute.</b> Two cases:\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>Skip latch set:</b> discharge it (§5.2), and the slot ends.</li><li><b>Otherwise:</b>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>fetch the byte at the PC and decode it;</li><li>if the byte is unlisted, apply §4.1;</li><li>otherwise fetch the operand (2-byte instructions only), from the step after the opcode;</li><li>move the SBM arm into the \"effective this instruction\" state and clear the arm;</li><li>compute \"next\" (one or two polynomial steps past the opcode);</li><li>execute;</li><li>record whether this instruction was a LAX;</li><li>set PC ← jump target if the instruction jumped, otherwise \"next\";</li><li>if a skip condition was satisfied, set the skip latch.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li><b>If W changed</b> during the instruction, re-sample the K flip-flop's input (§10.2) <b>and re-latch the wake line from it</b> (§7.3, the default <code>wakeRelatch: \"strobe\"</code>). This step belongs to the \"Otherwise\" branch of step 4 in effect: only an executed instruction can change W.</li></ol>\n<p>Because the ticks come before execution, <code>TF1</code>, <code>TF4</code> and <code>TIS</code> see the divider <b>at the end of their own cycle</b>, and an <code>IDIV</code> leaves the count at 0 at the end of its slot.</p>\n<p>A <b>halted</b> slot advances the oscillator 2 ticks and then tests the wake condition. Nothing else happens (§7).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">5.2 Skips</h5>\n<p>A satisfied skip condition does not move the PC. Instead it <b>sets a latch</b>, and the <b>next slot</b> is spent retiring the skipped instruction [MECHANISM, <code>SKIP_COSTS_A_CYCLE</code>]. That slot does the following:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Fetch the first byte at the PC and decode it <b>for its width only</b>: 2 for <code>LBL</code>, <code>TL</code> and <code>TML</code>; 1 for everything else; 1 for an unlisted byte.</li><li>Advance P_L by that many polynomial steps. A skip therefore skips the <b>whole</b> instruction, both bytes of a 2-byte instruction included, and an operand byte is never decoded as an opcode [MECHANISM, <code>SKIP_SPANS_WHOLE_INSTRUCTION</code>].</li><li>Clear the LAX-succession flag. The retired word counts as a non-LAX.</li><li>Leave the SBM arm untouched, because this slot does no RAM addressing.</li><li>Spend one slot (2 ticks). <code>step()</code> returns the mnemonic <code>\"SKIP\"</code>.</li></ol>\n<p>The skipped instruction has <b>no effects</b>. A skipped <code>CEND</code> does not halt, a skipped <code>WR</code> or <code>WS</code> does not count in <code>_twCount</code>, and so on.</p>\n<p><b>After <code>RTN1</code></b> the skip applies at the <b>returned-to</b> address.</p>\n<p><b>What \"skip\" covers.</b> These satisfy a skip condition:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>the condition-tests <code>ADD11</code>, <code>ADX</code> (but not <code>DC</code>), <code>EXCI</code>, <code>EXCD</code>, <code>INCB</code>, <code>DECB</code>, <code>TB</code>, <code>TC</code>, <code>TAM</code>, <code>TMI</code>, <code>TIS</code>, <code>TA0</code>, <code>TABL</code>, <code>TAL</code>, <code>TF1</code> and <code>TF4</code>;</li><li><code>RTN1</code>, which always skips.</li></ul>\n<p>No other instruction skips.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">5.3 LAX in succession</h5>\n<p>A <code>LAX</code> takes effect only if the <b>previous executed instruction was not a LAX</b>.</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>In a run of consecutive LAX, <b>the first one loads</b> and the rest leave Acc unchanged, each still costing one slot.</li><li>A suppressed LAX still counts as \"a LAX\" for the one after it.</li><li>What resets the flag:\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>any executed non-LAX instruction;</li><li>a skip slot;</li><li>power-on (which clears it);</li><li>CEND, which is itself a non-LAX.</li></ul></li><li>An unlisted byte does <b>not</b> reset the flag (§4.1).</li></ul>\n<p>[NOT ESTABLISHED, <code>LAX_SUCCESSION</code>. Reason (INSTRUMENT + SOURCE-READ): one shipped image holds a table of four LAX entered at four points by its callers. Under \"first\" each entry loads its own constant; under \"last\" every entry loads the same constant and the table is pointless. Another emulator's LAX handler ignores a LAX whose predecessor was a LAX, and with \"first\" the image runs instruction-for-instruction with the reference port (CONVERGENCE). Board §24.1.]</p>\n<p><b>Rival (NOT SCORED):</b> <code>laxSuccession: \"last\"</code>, in which only the last LAX of a run loads.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">5.4 Width and time</h5>\n<p>Width and time are independent. A 2-byte instruction (<code>LBL</code>, <code>TL</code>, <code>TML</code>) costs <b>one</b> slot, and so does <code>TM</code> with its IDX read [MECHANISM: one row of [90] p157; [90] p149 F6 prints one instruction cycle with no per-byte qualifier]. Every slot is 2 ticks; there are no other durations.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">5.5 Stack</h5>\n<p>Two 12-bit registers form a <b>shift</b>, not an array [ADJUDICATED [86] p90 (two registers, S and R); [90] p157 prints the pop as a chain, P ← S ← R].</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>Push</b> (<code>TM</code>, <code>TML</code>): R ← S, then S ← the return site.</li><li><b>Pop</b> (<code>RTN0</code>, <code>RTN1</code>): P ← S, then S ← R. <b>R keeps its value.</b></li></ul>\n<p><b>A third nested call</b> overwrites R, so <b>the oldest return address is lost</b> [MECHANISM, <code>THIRD_NESTED_CALL</code>]. The returns then behave like this:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>the innermost return goes to S;</li><li>the next return goes to the old R, the second-level site;</li><li>every further return goes to that <b>same</b> second-level site again, because the pop copies R into S and leaves R in place.</li></ul>\n<p>See self-check vector §14.4.</p>\n<p>The return site is always the step after the <b>whole</b> call instruction: 1 step for <code>TM</code>, 2 for <code>TML</code> [MECHANISM, <code>RETURN_ADDRESS_SPANS_OPERAND</code>]. With an empty stack (0:0:0) a return lands on the cover page and executes there as code (§3.5).</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s6\">6. Reset (ACL) state</h4>\n<p>The ABI's <code>reset()</code> is the power-on/ACL path. It does all of the following, with <b>no 0.5 s settling</b> (the core's <code>settle</code> option is off at the ABI):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value after <code>reset()</code></th><th>Tier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PC</td><td><b>P_U = 3, P_M = 7, P_L = 0</b>, so ABI <code>pc</code> = <b>0xDC0</b></td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p153 (2), p154 (7), p153 Fig. 1 \"Power on\"</td></tr><tr><td>Stack S, R</td><td>0:0:0, 0:0:0</td><td>MECHANISM (zeroed); NOT PRINTED [90] p149–p158</td></tr><tr><td>RAM</td><td>all 128 nibbles 0</td><td>MECHANISM; NOT PRINTED</td></tr><tr><td>B_M, B_L</td><td>0, 0</td><td>MECHANISM; NOT PRINTED</td></tr><tr><td>Acc, C, W, L, Y, R</td><td>all 0</td><td>MECHANISM; NOT PRINTED</td></tr><tr><td><b>BP</b></td><td><b>1</b> (display on)</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED (<code>BP_TO_COMMONS</code>). Reason (SOURCE-READ + INSTRUMENT): another emulator's device reset sets BP = 1, and 7 shipped SM510 images never execute <code>ATBP</code> in a 40 s attract run yet the reference port lights all of them. With BP = 0 at ACL those seven stay dark forever.</td></tr><tr><td>BC</td><td>0 (display mode)</td><td>MECHANISM: the printed display-mode value of [90] p152 (5)</td></tr><tr><td>SBM arm, skip latch, LAX flag</td><td>clear</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td>Divider count, gamma</td><td>0, 0; tick counter 0</td><td>MECHANISM; NOT PRINTED for SM510</td></tr><tr><td>halted</td><td>0</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td>K flip-flop input (<code>kPending</code>)</td><td>re-sampled with W = 0: equals the <code>inFixedRow</code> row if <code>inFixedRow</code> ≥ 0, else 0</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td>wake line</td><td>(<code>kPending</code> ≠ 0)</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>_twCount</code>, <code>_cendCount</code></td><td>0</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td>LCD plane</td><td>recomputed from this state (§9)</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td>Input wiring (<code>inRows</code>, <code>inFixedRow</code>)</td><td><b>kept</b>; reset does not clear the board</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The K flip-flop <b>output</b> is not cleared by reset. It is overwritten from <code>kPending</code> at the start of the first slot, so this cannot be observed.</p>\n<p><b>ACL settling.</b> [90] p154 (7) prints \"about 0.5 sec\" from oscillator start to program start, and the constant <code>ACL_SETTLING_SECONDS</code> = 0.5 carries it. The core can pre-run the divider for 16,384 ticks (<code>powerOn({settle:true})</code>), but <b>the ABI does not</b>. At the ABI the divider starts at 0 when the first instruction runs, and the first gamma edge falls at tick 32,768 (§8).</p>\n<p><b>Halt-wake vector.</b> 1:0:0, giving ABI <code>pc</code> = <b>0x400</b> [ADJUDICATED [90] p154 bullet 1 and (5); [90] p153 Fig. 1 \"Start from CEND\"]. This is a <b>different</b> vector from power-on.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s7\">7. Standby, halt and wake</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">7.1 CEND</h5>\n<p><code>CEND</code> sets <b>halted</b>. In every following slot, the system clock is stopped but the crystal and divider run [ADJUDICATED [90] p154 (5) \"halting almost all the system clocks\"]. The 1S wake source is the divider's own output, so the divider must run during halt [MECHANISM]. <b>A halted slot costs the same 2 ticks as a running one</b> [MECHANISM: a halted slot that cost nothing froze 4 of 18 images under the site's drive].</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">7.2 Wake condition</h5>\n<p>At the <b>end of each halted slot</b>, after its 2 ticks, the core tests:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p>wake if halted AND (gamma = 1 OR wake line = 1)</p></div>\n<p>[ADJUDICATED sources [90] p154 (5): \"γ F/F must be reset [read: set] or one or more inputs of K1–K4 must go High\"; [90] p154 bullet 1: \"by a 1S signal or a key input signal\".]</p>\n<p>A wake does the following:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>halted ← 0;</li><li>PC ← <b>1:0:0</b>;</li><li>the stack, RAM and all registers are untouched;</li><li><b>gamma is not cleared by the wake</b>, and keeps its value until the program executes <code>TIS</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>Consequences [MECHANISM]:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>gamma pending at CEND.</b> If gamma is already 1 when <code>CEND</code> executes (it was set and not yet cleared by <code>TIS</code>, or it was set by the ticks of CEND's own slot), the part wakes at the end of the very next slot.</li><li><b>Held key.</b> If the wake line is 1 at CEND (a key still held, as last latched), the part likewise wakes after one halted slot. A program that re-executes CEND while a key is held wakes every second slot (§14.6).</li><li><b>Wake line during halt.</b> No instruction runs, so W cannot change while halted. The wake line changes only when the site calls <code>updateKLine()</code>, and if that raises it the part wakes at once (§7.3).</li></ul>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">7.3 The wake-latch rule: <code>wakeRelatch: \"strobe\"</code></h5>\n<p>The wake line is (<code>kPending</code> ≠ 0), <b>re-latched whenever <code>kPending</code> is re-sampled</b>: by <code>updateKLine()</code> (an input change on the site), by <code>reset()</code>, and by the program's own strobe writes. A <code>WR</code> or <code>WS</code> that puts out a column on which a key is held raises the wake line in that same slot, so a <code>CEND</code> that follows wakes after one halted slot (§14.6).</p>\n<p>[MECHANISM. [90] p154 s(5): the system clock restarts when \"one or more INPUTS of K1-K4\" go High. A K input is the level a closed key puts on it through the strobe column being driven, so a strobe write that selects a held key raises the input exactly as a press does. Matched by observing the reference emulator's outputs (RAM per frame under the same drive): with this rule a Game A press in the second after the opening Time tap starts a game on Mario's Cement Factory, Donkey Kong Jr., Crab Grab, DK Hockey, Donkey Kong 3 and the Table Top Cement Factory, as the reference does, and Oil Panic after game over settles to the reference's RAM activity (about 20 to 40 changes per 10 s, not 350). Shared with SM511/512 (<code>30-SM511-SM512.md</code> §10).]</p>\n<p><b>Rival (NOT SCORED):</b> <code>wakeRelatch: \"external\"</code>, the old site engine's convention, which this surface used until 28 Oct 2026. The wake line is re-latched only by <code>updateKLine()</code> and <code>reset()</code>, never by a strobe write, so whether a key held across a <code>CEND</code> wakes the part depends on which column was out at the instant of the press. Under it <code>updateKLine()</code> never wakes the part by itself; the wake is taken by the next <code>step()</code>. It is the acceptance suite's broken build <code>rival-sm510-wake</code>.</p>\n<p>At the ABI, <code>updateKLine()</code> <b>wakes the part at once</b> when it raises the wake line of a halted part; the next <code>step()</code> then runs the instruction at 1:0:0.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">7.4 The WAKE-SAME-SLOT convention of <code>step()</code></h5>\n<p>The ABI's <code>step()</code> runs one core slot. If that slot was halted and ended with a wake, <code>step()</code> <b>immediately runs a second slot</b>: K clock, 2 ticks, and the first instruction at 1:0:0. So:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>a non-waking halted <code>step()</code> returns null, costs 2 ticks and leaves <code>halt</code> = 1;</li><li><b>a waking <code>step()</code> costs 4 ticks</b> (2 halted + 2 running), executes the instruction at 0x400, and leaves <code>pc</code> past it;</li><li><code>_twCount</code> and <code>_cendCount</code> count the second slot's instruction if it is WR, WS or CEND.</li></ul>\n<p>[CONVERGENCE. Reason: the site paces frames by counting <code>step()</code> calls, and the reference port wakes and executes in one pass. A core needing one extra call per wake drifted off frame parity on 18 of 18 titles (<code>tools/site_parity.mjs</code>). Oscillator time is unchanged; only the call boundary moves. Board §26.3.]</p>\n<p>A core used <b>outside</b> the ABI resumes in the next slot instead. That is NOT SCORED.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">7.5 Interaction with skip, SBM and LAX</h5>\n<p><code>CEND</code> arms no skip. A pending SBM arm is consumed by CEND itself. The LAX flag is cleared by CEND (a non-LAX). After a wake the first instruction therefore sees no skip, no SBM and no LAX predecessor.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s8\">8. Divider and the one-second flag</h4>\n<p><b>The divider</b> is a 15-stage binary counter clocked by the 32,768 Hz crystal [ADJUDICATED [86] p90 <code>DIV(15)</code>, [90] p154 (4)]. The core holds it as <code>count</code> in 0–32767 and adds 1 per tick, modulo 32768. It runs in every slot, halted or not.</p>\n<p><b>gamma (1S)</b> is set on the tick where the count <b>wraps from 32767 to 0</b>. That is the last stage's 1 → 0 transition, which happens once per 32,768 ticks = once per second. [ADJUDICATED that gamma is set by the 1S edge ([90] p154 (4)). Which polarity of the last stage is \"1S\" is NOT ESTABLISHED; the core takes the wrap. Reason (SOURCE-READ + CONVERGENCE): Another emulator sets gamma on the divider overflow. Under the old 0 → 1 reading the first gamma after <code>IDIV</code> came after half a second, and the core diverged from the reference port 0.5 s after each IDIV. With the wrap, 18 of 18 SM510 images run instruction-for-instruction with the reference port. Board §24.1.] <b>Rival (NOT SCORED):</b> <code>gammaEdge: \"msb-rise\"</code>, which sets gamma on bit 14 rising (count 16383 → 16384).</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>gamma clear:</b> only <code>TIS</code>, which clears unconditionally. Neither a wake nor <code>IDIV</code> clears it.</li><li><b><code>IDIV</code>:</b> count ← 0. This does <b>not</b> set gamma (it is not a tick) and does not clear it. The next gamma falls 32,768 ticks after the end of the IDIV slot.</li><li><b>Divider-reading instructions:</b></li></ul>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instruction</th><th>Reads</th><th>Frequency</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>TF1</code></td><td>count bit 14</td><td>1 Hz</td><td>skip if 1</td></tr><tr><td><code>TF4</code></td><td>count bit 11</td><td>8 Hz</td><td>skip if 1</td></tr><tr><td><code>TIS</code></td><td>gamma</td><td>none</td><td>skip if 0; gamma ← 0</td></tr><tr><td><code>IDIV</code></td><td>none</td><td>none</td><td>count ← 0</td></tr><tr><td>(R pins)</td><td>count bit 2</td><td>4,096 Hz</td><td>§11</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>[<code>DIVIDER_TAPS_TF1_TF4</code>, NOT ESTABLISHED. The numbering runs from the output end, so f*n* is count bit (15 − <em>n</em>). The constraint is SM500's <code>DTA</code> (f1 in Acc3, the most significant position) on [90] p126–127. <b>Rival (NOT SCORED):</b> <code>dividerNumbering: \"from-input\"</code>, under which f1 is bit 0 (16,384 Hz).]</p>\n<p>All of these read the count <b>after</b> their slot's 2 ticks (§5.1). From reset with no IDIV, the count at the end of slot <em>k</em> (1-based) is 2*k* mod 32768, and gamma first becomes 1 during slot 16,384.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s9\">9. Display</h4>\n<p><b>Drive scheme.</b> 1/4 duty, 1/3 bias. There are four commons H1–H4 (index <em>h</em> = 0–3) and segment pins a1–a16, b1–b16 and one bs [ADJUDICATED [90] p154 (3), [86] p91; [90] p149 F7 \"34 bits\" is erratum E5 for 33]. On common <em>h</em>:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>pin a*(w+1)* carries bit <em>h</em> of RAM[0x60 + *w*];</li><li>pin b*(w+1)* carries bit <em>h</em> of RAM[0x70 + *w*];</li><li>bs carries bit <em>h</em> of <b>L</b>.</li></ul>\n<p>[bs ← L: NOT ESTABLISHED (<code>BS_AND_L</code>). On SM510 the Y latch <b>does not</b> flash or gate bs; nothing reads Y.]</p>\n<p><b>Common gate.</b> All four commons are enabled together <b>iff BC = 0 and (BP AND 1) = 1</b>. Bits 1–3 of BP are stored but never consulted. [NOT ESTABLISHED (<code>BP_TO_COMMONS</code>). Reason (SOURCE-READ + INSTRUMENT): [90] p131 says the SM5A's BP turns the display on and off; another emulator's LCD row read blanks on BP bit 0 alone; across the 18 SM510 images the only values ever written to BP are 0 and 1, and the old per-common reading left H2–H4 dark on all 11 titles that draw. Board §24.1. The printed condition \"BP = 1 and BC = 0\" is ADJUDICATED [90] p152 (5).] <b>Rivals (NOT SCORED):</b> <code>bpRule: \"per-common\"</code> (BP bit <em>h</em> enables common <em>h</em>) and <code>bpAtAcl: 0</code>.</p>\n<p>When the gate is off, every segment of every common reads 0, bs included.</p>\n<p><b>The ABI plane.</b> <code>lcd</code> is a 12-entry array of 16-bit words. <code>updateLcd()</code> (and <code>reset()</code>) recomputes it from the current state, and nothing else does. Entry index = <b>(A × 4) + C</b>, where C = common <em>h</em> (0–3):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>A</th><th>Lane</th><th>Bit B of entry (A×4)+C</th><th>Tier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>segment group <code>a</code> = file R</td><td>bit C of RAM[0x60 + B], B = 0–15</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>segment group <code>b</code> = file S</td><td>bit C of RAM[0x70 + B], B = 0–15</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>flashing pins</td><td>B = 0: bit C of L; B = 1: <b>always 0 on SM510</b> (that is X, which exists only on SM511/512); B = 2–15: 0</td><td>MECHANISM; L→bs NOT ESTABLISHED</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Every entry is 0 when the gate is off. The site renders segment <code>A.B.C</code> as bit B of <code>lcd[(A &lt;&lt; 2) | C]</code>, and <code>LCD_C_BITS</code> = 2.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> RAM[0x60] = 1 with BP = 1 and BC = 0 gives <code>lcd[0]</code> = 0x0001 (segment <code>0.0.0</code>). Executing <code>SC</code> then <code>BDC</code> makes all 12 entries 0 at the next <code>updateLcd()</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Phase.</b> The plane is the <b>instantaneous</b> content, not a time-multiplexed scan. The frame rate (64 Hz, [90] p156 Fig. 10) and the optical model belong to another chapter.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s10\">10. Input</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">10.1 Pins and the matrix</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Pin</th><th>Direction</th><th>Unconnected level</th><th>At the ABI</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>K1–K4</td><td>in</td><td><b>pull-down</b> (0)</td><td>from <code>inRows</code> / <code>inFixedRow</code> via the flip-flop</td></tr><tr><td>beta</td><td>in</td><td><b>pull-up</b> (1)</td><td><b>1</b>, or 0 while <code>testContacts.B</code> = 0</td></tr><tr><td>BA</td><td>in</td><td><b>pull-up</b> (1)</td><td><b>1</b>, or 0 while <code>testContacts.BA</code> = 0</td></tr><tr><td>S1–S8</td><td>out</td><td>none</td><td>= W bits 0–7</td></tr><tr><td>ACL</td><td>in</td><td>pull-down</td><td><code>reset()</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>[ADJUDICATED [86] p91 pin table; <code>BETA_AND_BA_DEFAULT</code>, <code>KEY_MATRIX_MODEL</code>]</p>\n<p><b>beta and BA at the ABI.</b> The ABI's <code>inB</code>/<code>inBA</code> setters store the value, and the getters return what was stored (initially 1). The core's beta and BA <b>stay at 1</b> whatever is written, so <code>TB</code> and <code>TAL</code> always skip, <b>unless the factory test contacts are closed</b> through the extra member <code>testContacts</code> (60-ABI.md §3, added on 2026-10-28): <code>cpu.testContacts = { B, BA }</code> drives beta and BA to the given levels (0 = the pad closed), <code>null</code> returns both to 1. The site writes it every frame, <code>null</code> unless a player has switched a contact on in the Cheats menu; with every contact open the machine runs exactly as before (INSTRUMENT: every one of the 61 units run against the previous bundle on a virtual clock, picture and sound identical, 2026-10-28). [ADJUDICATED that beta and BA are pull-up inputs tested by TB and TAL, [86] p91; that the pads ARE these two pins on the G&amp;W boards is SOURCE-READ (another emulator's board driver, PROVENANCE §5.7.1); what each closed pad does in a given program is CONVERGENCE, its output observed with the same drive.] [CONVERGENCE. Reason: the reference port's SM510 TB/TAL skip unconditionally. Forwarding the site's hammer writes made TB/TAL diverge as soon as a hammer was held, which was the first gameplay divergence on 6 of 18 titles (<code>tools/rout_parity.mjs</code>). Board §27.1.] <b>Rival (NOT SCORED):</b> <code>coreCfg.sitePins: true</code>, which forwards the writes.</p>\n<p><b>Key sample.</b> The K value the matrix presents is:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p>K = (directK OR the OR, over every <em>i</em> in 0–7 with W bit <em>i</em> = 1, of matrix[*i*]) AND 0xF</p></div>\n<p>Bit <em>j</em> of K is line K(*j*+1), and lands in Acc bit <em>j</em>. [MECHANISM, <code>KEY_MATRIX_MODEL</code>.]</p>\n<p><b>How <code>inRows</code> and <code>inFixedRow</code> map onto it.</b> This mapping is applied whenever either member is set:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b><code>inFixedRow</code> ≥ 0:</b> directK ← inRows[inFixedRow] AND 0xF, and every matrix entry ← 0. The row is read with <b>no</b> strobe gating.</li><li><b><code>inFixedRow</code> &lt; 0</b> (the default is −1): directK ← 0, and matrix[*i*] ← inRows[*i*] AND 0xF for <em>i</em> &lt; min(8, length). Every other entry is 0.</li></ul>\n<p>The default <code>inRows</code> is [0], so with nothing set no key ever reads. <b>Setting <code>inRows</code> or <code>inFixedRow</code> does not re-sample K.</b> The new value is seen only after <code>updateKLine()</code> or a W change (§10.2).</p>\n<p><code>rMuxInvert</code> reads false. <b>Setting it truthy throws</b> (an <code>AbiRefusal</code>), because it is inert on SM510. Setting it false is accepted.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">10.2 The K flip-flop and its sample point</h5>\n<p>K is a 4-bit flip-flop [ADJUDICATED [86] p90 and [90] p150 symbol key \"K : Key input F/F\"; [90] p152 (1) \"be sure to take the interval at least 1 step between strobe outputs and K inputs\"; <code>K_LINE_SAMPLE_POINT</code>]. The core keeps two nodes.</p>\n<p><b><code>kPending</code>, the flip-flop's input.</b> It is set to the key sample (§10.1), using the W of that moment, at four points:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>at the <b>end of a slot whose instruction changed W</b>. A WR or WS that leaves W numerically unchanged (for example <code>WR</code> with W = 0) does not re-sample;</li><li>on each ABI <code>updateKLine()</code>;</li><li>on <code>reset()</code>;</li><li>at construction.</li></ol>\n<p><b><code>kLatch</code>, the flip-flop's output.</b> It ← kPending at the <b>start of every running slot</b>, skip slots and locked slots included, but not halted slots. <code>KTA</code> reads kLatch.</p>\n<p><b>Net observable rule</b> (what you must reproduce): <code>KTA</code> returns the key sample taken at the most recent of (a strobe-changing instruction's end, <code>updateKLine()</code>, <code>reset()</code>). In particular, a <code>KTA</code> in the slot <b>immediately after</b> a <code>WS</code>/<code>WR</code> that changed W sees the <b>new</b> column, as §14.5 shows. A <code>KTA</code> does not see an <code>inRows</code> change until <code>updateKLine()</code> or a W change re-samples.</p>\n<p><b>The wake path</b> reads the wake line, not kLatch (§7.3).</p>\n<p><b>Rival (NOT SCORED):</b> <code>kSamplePoint: \"live\"</code>. Under it <code>KTA</code> evaluates the matrix at the moment it executes, and the wake line is never refreshed.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s11\">11. Sound</h4>\n<p><b>The R latch.</b> <code>ATR</code> writes R ← Acc AND 3, with R1 = bit 0 and R2 = bit 1 [NOT ESTABLISHED, <code>ATR_WHICH_BITS</code>; rival: the high two bits]. The ABI member <code>r</code> returns this raw latch.</p>\n<p><b>The R-pin level (<code>rOut</code>).</b> The port is in buzzer mode [<code>BUZZER_PORT_MODE</code> default \"tone\", NOT ESTABLISHED; [90] p152 (6) prints the mask-option alternative]. The tone is the divider's <b>bit 2</b>: 32,768 ÷ 8 = <b>4,096 Hz</b>, three halvings, half-period 4 ticks [ADJUDICATED frequency [90] p155 (8) and [90] p169; \"kHz\" there is a unit erratum]. Let:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><em>count</em> = the divider count <b>at the moment <code>rOut</code> is read</b>: after the last slot's ticks, and after an IDIV in that slot;</li><li><em>t</em> = bit 2 of <em>count</em>;</li><li><em>R1</em>, <em>R2</em> = the two bits of the R latch as of now.</li></ul>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p>pin1 = R1 AND <em>t</em> pin2 = R2 AND (NOT <em>t</em>) <b>rOut = pin1 + 2 × pin2</b></p></div>\n<p>Each latch bit gates its own pin, and pin 2 carries the <b>inverted</b> phase. So:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>R = 3 produces the anti-phase pair (\"Volume MAX.\");</li><li>R = 1 produces pin 1 alone (\"Volume MIN.\");</li><li>R = 0 is silent.</li></ul>\n<p>[NOT ESTABLISHED, <code>BUZZER_R_BIT_ROLES</code>, default \"gated-antiphase\". Reason (SOURCE-READ, a formula read as a fact): another emulator's SM510 buzzer output computes the same two facts (tap on divider bit 2; pin 2 anti-phase, both gated). INSTRUMENT: identical to the reference port's per-instruction R level after every one of 11,839,980 steps on 18 of 18 titles (<code>tools/rout_parity.mjs</code>, board §27.1). Controls that red it: a tap one stage lower reds 18/18; pin 2 in phase reds 18/18; a tap on a free-running tick count instead of the divider reds 14/18.] <b>Rivals (NOT SCORED):</b> <code>buzzerBitRoles: \"levels\"</code> and <code>rPortMode: \"latch\"</code>, either of which gives rOut = R.</p>\n<p><b>Consequences:</b></p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>From reset the count is always even at a <code>step()</code> boundary, so with R = 3, rOut alternates 2, 2, 1, 1, … every two slots.</li><li><code>IDIV</code> re-phases the tone, because it clears the same counter.</li><li>During halt, rOut keeps toggling if R ≠ 0.</li><li><code>rOut</code> cannot be written: setting it throws.</li></ul>\n<p><b><code>_twCount</code> / <code>_cendCount</code>.</b> These are ABI counters, zeroed by <code>reset()</code>:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><code>_twCount</code> counts <code>WR</code>/<code>WS</code> instructions actually executed;</li><li><code>_cendCount</code> counts <code>CEND</code> instructions actually executed.</li></ul>\n<p>Skipped words and unlisted bytes never count. In a waking <code>step()</code> the counted instruction is the one at the wake vector (§7.4). [MECHANISM.]</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s12\">12. Registered choices</h4>\n<p>These are all 31 keys of <code>src/unestablished.mjs</code> <code>CHOICES</code>, followed by the unregistered options the adapter or core sets. \"Default\" is what the ABI runs.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Default (scored)</th><th>Rival (NOT SCORED)</th><th>Tier</th><th>Reason / note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>P_L_SEQUENCE</code></td><td>right shift, feedback NOT(b0 XOR b1) into bit 5 (§3.2)</td><td>the other 11 structurally valid rules; named rival: left shift, taps 0b110000, XNOR</td><td>INSTRUMENT</td><td>0 of 773 programmed pages untileable under the default; the next best leaves 43</td></tr><tr><td><code>TM_BYTE_COUNT</code></td><td>TM is 1 byte</td><td>2 bytes</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>63 one-byte IDX entries fit a 63-step page; two-byte entries would not</td></tr><tr><td><code>IDX_STRUCTURE</code></td><td>IDX is the 1-byte word stored in the cover page (P_U ← b7–6, P_L ← b5–0, P_M ← 4), not an opcode</td><td>IDX as a first-byte opcode</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>as an opcode it overlaps 239 of 256 encodings</td></tr><tr><td><code>COVER_PAGE_IS_IDX</code></td><td>page (0,0) is read by TM as a table (§3.5)</td><td>TM jumps into (0,0) and it executes as code</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>[90] p153 Fig. 1 label; the only 12 untileable pages in the corpus are all this page</td></tr><tr><td><code>LB_HIGH_NIBBLE</code></td><td>OR: B_L3 = B_L2 = I3 OR I2</td><td>\"literal\" XOR; \"plain\" (B_L3,2 = I3,I2)</td><td>INSTRUMENT</td><td>RAM-clear loops seed B_L with <code>LB 0x4C</code> and count down, which gives 16 words under OR and 4 under XOR; another emulator's LB handler agrees (SOURCE-READ). Board §24.1</td></tr><tr><td><code>LB_BM2</code></td><td>LB preserves B_M2</td><td>clear B_M2</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>an unnamed destination bit is not written (as for ATPL's P_L5–4)</td></tr><tr><td><code>LAX_SUCCESSION</code></td><td>the FIRST LAX of a run loads</td><td>the LAST loads</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED</td><td>a shipped LAX table entered at four points works only under FIRST; another emulator's LAX handler (SOURCE-READ); reference-port lockstep (CONVERGENCE)</td></tr><tr><td><code>DC_SKIP_BEHAVIOUR</code></td><td>0x3A never skips</td><td>skips like ADX</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p126</td><td>registered for visibility</td></tr><tr><td><code>DIVIDER_TAPS_TF1_TF4</code></td><td>TF1 = bit 14 (1 Hz), TF4 = bit 11 (8 Hz)</td><td>numbering from the input end (f1 = 16,384 Hz)</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED</td><td>SM500 <code>DTA</code> puts f1 in the MSB ([90] p126–127)</td></tr><tr><td><code>ATR_WHICH_BITS</code></td><td>R1 = Acc0, R2 = Acc1</td><td>the high two bits</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED</td><td>[90] p157 \"Ri ← Acc, i = 1, 2\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>BP_TO_COMMONS</code></td><td>BP bit 0 gates all four commons; ACL BP = 1</td><td>bit <em>h</em> gates common <em>h</em>; ACL BP = 0</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED</td><td>SOURCE-READ (another emulator's LCD row read and device reset) + INSTRUMENT (programs write only 0/1; 7 titles never write BP)</td></tr><tr><td><code>BS_AND_L</code></td><td>bs = bit <em>h</em> of L on common <em>h</em></td><td>another driver</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED</td><td>L is the 4-bit latch with no other destination</td></tr><tr><td><code>ATFC_DESTINATION</code></td><td>Y drives nothing</td><td>Y as a melody/flash control (carried from SM511)</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED</td><td>no Y pin on [86] p91</td></tr><tr><td><code>THIRD_NESTED_CALL</code></td><td>the oldest return address (R) is lost</td><td>saturating stack</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>two printed registers; a shift drops what falls off</td></tr><tr><td><code>SKIP_COSTS_A_CYCLE</code></td><td>a skipped instruction costs one slot</td><td>free skip</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>learning the width needs a fetch+decode, which is a cycle</td></tr><tr><td><code>SKIP_SPANS_WHOLE_INSTRUCTION</code></td><td>skip both bytes of a 2-byte instruction</td><td>skip one step</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>an operand byte is never decoded as an opcode</td></tr><tr><td><code>RETURN_ADDRESS_SPANS_OPERAND</code></td><td>push = the step after the whole call (TM +1, TML +2 polynomial steps)</td><td>arithmetic +1 / the operand step</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>same rule as the skip</td></tr><tr><td><code>ATPL_ORDER</code></td><td>no polynomial step after ATPL; P_L5–4 kept, P_L3–0 ← Acc</td><td>write-then-step; step-then-write</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p154</td><td>ATPL is in the printed jump list</td></tr><tr><td><code>PAGE_WRAP</code></td><td>no carry into P_M; the page is a closed 63-cycle</td><td>carry into P_M</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>the counter's orbit is closed</td></tr><tr><td><code>INSTRUCTION_TIMING</code></td><td>2 oscillator ticks per slot</td><td>any other ratio</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p149 F6</td><td>61 µs = 1/16,384 Hz</td></tr><tr><td><code>KEY_MATRIX_MODEL</code></td><td>K = OR over driven strobes of matrix columns, plus directK</td><td>K read with no strobe</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>[86] p91–92</td></tr><tr><td><code>BETA_AND_BA_DEFAULT</code></td><td>pull-up: both read 1</td><td>low</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p91</td><td>at the ABI they are also held at 1 (§10.1)</td></tr><tr><td><code>PANEL_OPTICAL_TIME_CONSTANTS</code></td><td>one frame period</td><td>larger or fitted</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED</td><td>display-model chapter; no ABI observable here</td></tr><tr><td><code>PANEL_THRESHOLD_RULE</code></td><td>geometric mean</td><td>arithmetic mean</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>display-model chapter; no ABI observable</td></tr><tr><td><code>SPEAKER_DELIVERY_FLOOR</code></td><td>96.3 dB</td><td>24-bit floor</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>audio chapter; no ABI observable</td></tr><tr><td><code>SPEAKER_DC_CORNER</code></td><td>20 Hz</td><td>a fixed pole</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>audio chapter; no ABI observable</td></tr><tr><td><code>SPEAKER_TRANSDUCER_RESPONSE</code></td><td>flat</td><td>resonant</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED</td><td>audio chapter; no ABI observable</td></tr><tr><td><code>K_LINE_SAMPLE_POINT</code></td><td>latched flip-flop (§10.2)</td><td><code>kSamplePoint: \"live\"</code></td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p90, [90] p152 (1)</td><td>recorded fix</td></tr><tr><td><code>R_ROW_SELECT_POLARITY</code></td><td>SM5A only; inert on SM510</td><td>none</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td><td>SM5A chapter</td></tr><tr><td><code>BUZZER_PORT_MODE</code></td><td>\"tone\"</td><td>\"latch\" (mask option)</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED</td><td>[90] p152 (6); per-device and not recorded in the program</td></tr><tr><td><code>BUZZER_R_BIT_ROLES</code></td><td>\"gated-antiphase\" (§11)</td><td>\"levels\"</td><td>NOT ESTABLISHED</td><td>SOURCE-READ formula + INSTRUMENT 18/18 rOut parity</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Unregistered options that are fixed at the ABI.</b> These have no <code>CHOICES</code> row, and you must still match them:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>ABI value</th><th>Rival (NOT SCORED)</th><th>Tier</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>wakeRelatch</code></td><td>\"strobe\" (§7.3, the core's own default)</td><td>\"external\" (the old site engine's)</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>[90] p154 s(5); matched to the reference emulator's outputs</td></tr><tr><td>waking <code>step()</code> runs the vector instruction</td><td>yes (§7.4)</td><td>resume in the next call</td><td>CONVERGENCE</td><td>frame parity 18/18; board §26.3</td></tr><tr><td><code>gammaEdge</code></td><td>\"wrap\" (§8)</td><td>\"msb-rise\"</td><td>SOURCE-READ + CONVERGENCE</td><td>board §24.1</td></tr><tr><td>divider-read phase</td><td>ticks before execution (§5.1)</td><td>ticks after</td><td>CONVERGENCE</td><td>board §24.1</td></tr><tr><td><code>sitePins</code></td><td>false: beta and BA held at 1</td><td>true</td><td>CONVERGENCE</td><td>board §27.1</td></tr><tr><td><code>stopOnUnlisted</code></td><td>false (§4.1)</td><td>true</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td><code>settle</code> on reset</td><td>off (§6)</td><td>0.5 s pre-run</td><td>MECHANISM</td><td>the ABI reset is called without it</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s13\">13. Constants</h4>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Identifier</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Tier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>POWER_ON_VECTOR</code></td><td>{pu 3, pm 7, pl 0}</td><td>ACL start address (ABI pc 0xDC0)</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p154 (7)</td></tr><tr><td><code>HALT_WAKE_VECTOR</code></td><td>{pu 1, pm 0, pl 0}</td><td>CEND wake address (ABI pc 0x400)</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p154 (5)</td></tr><tr><td><code>PIN_DEFAULTS</code></td><td>{beta 1, ba 1, k 0, acl 0}</td><td>unconnected pin levels</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p91</td></tr><tr><td><code>LOCK_STATE</code></td><td>63 (0x3F)</td><td>P_L state outside the cycle</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p124</td></tr><tr><td><code>STEPS_PER_PAGE</code></td><td>63</td><td>steps in a page</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p164 Fig. 1</td></tr><tr><td><code>DEFAULT_RULE</code></td><td>{dir right, taps 3, inv true}</td><td>the P_L rule (§3.2)</td><td>INSTRUMENT</td></tr><tr><td><code>NAMED_RULES</code></td><td>source-read = {right, 3, XNOR}; builder = {left, 48, XNOR}</td><td>the two named rival rules</td><td>INSTRUMENT / MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>COVER_PAGE</code></td><td>{pu 0, pm 0}</td><td>subroutine cover page</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p153 Fig. 1</td></tr><tr><td><code>EVENT</code></td><td><code>UNLISTED \"unlisted-encoding\"</code>, <code>LOCKED \"p_l-locked\"</code>, <code>OUT_OF_PROGRAM \"fetch-past-end-of-program\"</code>, <code>HALTED \"clock-stopped\"</code>, <code>COVER_PAGE_EXECUTED \"cover-page-entered-as-code\"</code></td><td>internal event names (not ABI-observable)</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>UNLISTED_CODES</code></td><td>[0x50, 0x5C, 0x7B, 0xFF]</td><td>the four unlisted first bytes</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p157</td></tr><tr><td><code>FILES</code></td><td>8</td><td>RAM files</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p154 (3)</td></tr><tr><td><code>WORDS_PER_FILE</code></td><td>16</td><td>nibbles per file</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td><code>NIBBLES</code></td><td>128</td><td>RAM size</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td><code>BITS</code></td><td>512</td><td>RAM bits</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p154 (3)</td></tr><tr><td><code>FILE_NAMES</code></td><td>X Y Z M P Q R S</td><td>file names by B_M</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p154 Fig. 3</td></tr><tr><td><code>DISPLAY_FILES</code></td><td>[6, 7]</td><td>files wired to the segment pins</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p154 Fig. 3</td></tr><tr><td><code>OSCILLATOR_HZ</code></td><td>32768</td><td>crystal</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p91</td></tr><tr><td><code>DIVIDER_STAGES</code></td><td>15</td><td>divider length</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p90, [90] p154 (4)</td></tr><tr><td><code>INSTRUCTION_CYCLE_US</code></td><td>61</td><td>instruction cycle</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p149 F6</td></tr><tr><td><code>SYSTEM_CLOCK_HZ</code></td><td>16384</td><td>oscillator ÷ 2</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td><code>OSC_PER_INSTRUCTION</code></td><td>2</td><td>oscillator ticks per slot (= ABI <code>clkDiv</code>)</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p149</td></tr><tr><td><code>ACL_SETTLING_SECONDS</code></td><td>0.5</td><td>ACL release to program start (not applied at the ABI)</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p154 (7)</td></tr><tr><td><code>COMMONS</code></td><td>4</td><td>LCD commons (1/4 duty)</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p154 (3)</td></tr><tr><td><code>SEGMENT_GROUPS</code></td><td>[\"a\", \"b\"]</td><td>SM510 segment groups</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p91</td></tr><tr><td><code>SEGMENTS_PER_GROUP</code></td><td>16</td><td>a1–a16, b1–b16</td><td>ADJUDICATED</td></tr><tr><td><code>SEGMENT_LANE_NAMES</code></td><td>[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]</td><td>lane names; \"c\" is SM512 only</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p161</td></tr><tr><td><code>BS_PINS</code></td><td>{SM510 1, SM511 2, SM512 2}</td><td>flashing pins per part</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p91, [90] p161</td></tr><tr><td><code>BUZZER_HZ</code></td><td>4096</td><td>buzzer tone</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p155 (8)</td></tr><tr><td><code>BUZZER_DIVISOR</code></td><td>8</td><td>oscillator ÷ tone</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>BUZZER_DIVIDER_STAGE</code></td><td>3</td><td>log2 of the divisor (tone = count bit 2)</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>BUZZER_HALF_PERIOD_TICKS</code></td><td>4</td><td>ticks per half period</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>MELODY_STEPS</code></td><td>256</td><td>SM511/512 melody memory, not used by SM510</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p166</td></tr><tr><td><code>MELODY_WORD_BITS</code></td><td>6</td><td>SM511/512 only</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p166</td></tr><tr><td><code>TONE_LENGTHS_MS</code></td><td>[125, 62.5]</td><td>SM511/512 only</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p166</td></tr><tr><td><code>PRINTED_CLOCK_CYCLES</code></td><td>codes 2–13 → 15.5, 16.5, 17.5, 18.5, 19.5, 21, 22, 23.5, 25, 26.5, 28, 29.5</td><td>SM511/512 only</td><td>ADJUDICATED [90] p166 Fig. 5</td></tr><tr><td><code>UNPRINTED_NOTE_CODES</code></td><td>[0, 1, 14, 15]</td><td>SM511/512 only</td><td>ADJUDICATED (unassigned on [90] p166)</td></tr><tr><td><code>SITE_CPU_MEMBERS</code></td><td><code>_cendCount _twCount clkDiv halt inB inBA inFixedRow inRows lcd pc r rMuxInvert rOut ram reset step updateKLine updateLcd</code></td><td>the 18 ABI members</td><td>INSTRUMENT (<code>tools/abi_check.mjs</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>LCD_C_BITS</code></td><td>2</td><td>plane index = (A &lt;&lt; 2) | C</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>WAKE_RELATCH</code> (adapter)</td><td>\"strobe\"</td><td>the wake-latch rule every SM510/511/512 surface is built with (§7.3)</td><td>MECHANISM [90] p154 s(5)</td></tr><tr><td><code>LANE_A</code></td><td>{a 0, b 1, c 2}</td><td>lane → A</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>FLASH_LANE_A</code></td><td>2</td><td>A of the flashing-pin lane</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>FLASH_BIT</code></td><td>{L 0, X 1}</td><td>bit of A = 2 per latch</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>LCD_C_BITS_5A</code></td><td>1</td><td>SM5A plane, not used by SM510</td><td>SOURCE-READ (site interface)</td></tr><tr><td><code>SM5A_INSTRUCTION_US</code></td><td>61</td><td>SM5A only</td><td>ADJUDICATED [86] p74</td></tr><tr><td><code>SM5A_CLK_DIV</code></td><td>2</td><td>SM5A only</td><td>MECHANISM</td></tr><tr><td><code>SM5A_SITE_BANK_OF_C</code></td><td>[\"W\", \"WP\"]</td><td>SM5A only</td><td>SOURCE-READ (site interface)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>SOURCE-READ (site interface)</code> reason: these are the site renderer's own index convention for SM5A artwork, re-derived from <code>gnw.js</code> on every run by <code>tools/sm5a_display_check.mjs</code>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s14\">14. Self-check vectors</h4>\n<p>All the programs below are <b>synthetic</b>. \"Put\" means placing bytes at successive steps of a page in the P_L order of §3.2 (n = 0, 1, 2, …), at program index P_U × 1024 + P_M × 64 + P_L. Every other program byte is 0x00. Each row is the state <b>after</b> that ABI <code>step()</code> call. Each run begins with <code>reset()</code>, with the default <code>inRows</code> = [0] and <code>inFixedRow</code> = −1 unless the vector says otherwise.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">14.1 The P_L sequence</h5>\n<p>From <code>reset()</code> with a program image of all 0x00 (<code>SKIP</code>), the ABI <code>pc</code> after step <em>n</em> is 0xDC0 + P_L(<em>n</em>), with P_L(<em>n</em>) taken from §3.2. Expected values:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>steps 1–6: 0xDE0, 0xDF0, 0xDF8, 0xDFC, 0xDFE, 0xDDF;</li><li>step 63: 0xDC0 again.</li></ul>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">14.2 Skip, LAX, cover-page call, RTN1, IDIV, CEND and a 1S wake</h5>\n<p>The program at page 3:7, by position:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>n</th><th>Bytes</th><th>Instruction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0x29</td><td><code>LAX 9</code></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0x25</td><td><code>LAX 5</code> (suppressed)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0x38</td><td><code>ADX 8</code> (sum 17: Acc ← 1, skip)</td></tr><tr><td>3–4</td><td>0x70 0x00</td><td><code>TL</code> (2 bytes, skipped whole)</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0x67</td><td><code>SC</code></td></tr><tr><td>6–7</td><td>0x5F 0x60</td><td><code>LBL</code> (B_M ← 6, B_L ← 0)</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>0x10</td><td><code>EXC 0</code></td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>0x23</td><td><code>LAX 3</code></td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>0x61</td><td><code>ATR</code></td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>0xC5</td><td><code>TM</code> (slot 5)</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>0x5D</td><td><code>CEND</code> (will be skipped by RTN1)</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>0x65</td><td><code>IDIV</code></td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>0x5D</td><td><code>CEND</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Other bytes:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>cover page: program[0x005] = 0x8A, the IDX word (P_U 2, P_L 0x0A);</li><li>program[0x90A] = 0x6F (<code>RTN1</code>);</li><li>page 1:0, n = 0: 0x58 (<code>TIS</code>); n = 1: 0x5D (<code>CEND</code>).</li></ul>\n<p>Columns: rOut, ticks = total oscillator ticks, count = divider. The Acc, C, B_M, B_L and gamma columns are internal, given as an aid.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>step</th><th>retired</th><th>pc</th><th>halt</th><th>rOut</th><th>ticks</th><th>count</th><th>Acc</th><th>C</th><th>B_M</th><th>B_L</th><th>gamma</th><th>RAM[0x60]</th><th>lcd[0]</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>LAX</td><td>0xDE0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>9</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>LAX (suppressed)</td><td>0xDF0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>9</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>ADX</td><td>0xDF8</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>6</td><td>6</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>(skip slot over TL)</td><td>0xDFE</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>8</td><td>8</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>SC</td><td>0xDDF</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>10</td><td>10</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>LBL</td><td>0xDF7</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>12</td><td>12</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>EXC</td><td>0xDFB</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>14</td><td>14</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0x0001</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>LAX</td><td>0xDFD</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>16</td><td>16</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0x0001</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>ATR (R = 3)</td><td>0xDDE</td><td>0</td><td><b>2</b></td><td>18</td><td>18</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0x0001</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>TM → IDX → 2:4:0x0A</td><td>0x90A</td><td>0</td><td><b>1</b></td><td>20</td><td>20</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0x0001</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>RTN1 → 3:7:0x0F</td><td>0xDCF</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>22</td><td>22</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0x0001</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>(skip slot over CEND)</td><td>0xDE7</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>24</td><td>24</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0x0001</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>IDIV</td><td>0xDF3</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>26</td><td><b>0</b></td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0x0001</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>CEND</td><td>0xDF9</td><td><b>1</b></td><td>2</td><td>28</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0x0001</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>(halted, returns null)</td><td>0xDF9</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>30</td><td>4</td><td>…</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>0</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>(halted)</td><td>0xDF9</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>32</td><td>6</td><td>…</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>0</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>…</td><td>halted, 2 ticks each</td><td>0xDF9</td><td>1</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>16397</td><td><b>waking step: TIS at 0x400</b></td><td><b>0x420</b></td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td><b>32796</b></td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0 (set at tick 32794, cleared by TIS; TIS did not skip)</td><td>1</td><td>0x0001</td></tr><tr><td>16398</td><td>CEND</td><td>0x430</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>32798</td><td>4</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>0</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>16399</td><td>(halted)</td><td>0x430</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>32800</td><td>6</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>1S wake timeline in ticks:</b></p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>IDIV's slot ends at tick 26 with count 0.</li><li>The count wraps 32767 → 0 at tick 26 + 32768 = <b>32794</b>, inside the halted slot spanning ticks 32792 → 32794. gamma is set.</li><li>The wake test at the end of that slot fires.</li><li>The same <code>step()</code> then runs the TIS slot (ticks 32794 → 32796) at 0x400.</li><li>That waking call spans 4 ticks.</li></ul>\n<p><code>_cendCount</code> = 2 and <code>_twCount</code> = 0 at the end.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">14.3 Arithmetic, carry and a skip over a 2-byte instruction</h5>\n<p>Program at 3:7, by position:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>n</th><th>Bytes</th><th>Instruction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0–1</td><td>0x5F 0x00</td><td><code>LBL</code> (B_M 0, B_L 0)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0x29</td><td><code>LAX 9</code></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0x10</td><td><code>EXC</code></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0x28</td><td><code>LAX 8</code></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0x67</td><td><code>SC</code></td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0x09</td><td><code>ADD11</code></td></tr><tr><td>7–8</td><td>0x5F 0x7F</td><td><code>LBL</code> (skipped)</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>0x6B</td><td><code>ROT</code></td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>0x52</td><td><code>TC</code></td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>0x0A</td><td><code>COMA</code> (skipped)</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>0x3A</td><td><code>DC</code></td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>0x44</td><td><code>LB</code></td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>0x0B</td><td><code>EXBLA</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>step</th><th>retired</th><th>pc</th><th>Acc</th><th>C</th><th>B_L</th><th>RAM[0]</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>LBL</td><td>0xDF0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>LAX</td><td>0xDF8</td><td>9</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>EXC</td><td>0xDFC</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>LAX</td><td>0xDFE</td><td>8</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>SC</td><td>0xDDF</td><td>8</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>ADD11 (8+9+1 = 18)</td><td>0xDEF</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>skip slot (2-byte LBL)</td><td>0xDFB</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>ROT</td><td>0xDFD</td><td>9</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>TC (C = 0: skip)</td><td>0xDDE</td><td>9</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>skip slot (COMA)</td><td>0xDCF</td><td>9</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>DC (9+10 = 19, no skip)</td><td>0xDE7</td><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>LB 0x44</td><td>0xDF3</td><td>3</td><td>0</td><td><b>13</b></td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>EXBLA</td><td>0xDF9</td><td>13</td><td>0</td><td>3</td><td>9</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">14.4 The third nested call</h5>\n<p>Continue §14.3 with these instructions:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>page 3:7, n = 15–16: <code>TML</code> 0x7D 0x00 (call 0:1:0);</li><li>page 0:1: <code>TML</code> 0x7E 0x00, then <code>RTN0</code>;</li><li>page 0:2: <code>TML</code> 0x7F 0x00, then <code>RTN0</code>;</li><li>page 0:3: <code>RTN0</code>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>step</th><th>retired</th><th>pc</th><th>S</th><th>R</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>14</td><td>TML</td><td>0x040</td><td>3:7:0x2E</td><td>0:0:0</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>TML</td><td>0x080</td><td>0:1:0x30</td><td>3:7:0x2E</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>TML</td><td>0x0C0</td><td>0:2:0x30</td><td>0:1:0x30 (3:7:0x2E lost)</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>RTN0</td><td>0x0B0</td><td>0:1:0x30</td><td>0:1:0x30</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>RTN0</td><td>0x070</td><td>0:1:0x30</td><td>0:1:0x30</td></tr><tr><td>19+</td><td>RTN0</td><td>0x070 forever</td><td>0:1:0x30</td><td>0:1:0x30</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">14.5 The K sample point (KTA immediately after a strobe write)</h5>\n<p>Set <code>inRows</code> = [0b0101, 0b0010], then <code>reset()</code>. Program at 3:7: <code>WS</code>, <code>KTA</code>, <code>WR</code>, <code>KTA</code>, <code>KTA</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>step</th><th>retired</th><th>W</th><th>Acc</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>WS</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>KTA</td><td>1</td><td><b>5</b></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>WR</td><td>2</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>KTA</td><td>2</td><td><b>2</b></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>KTA</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">14.6 Key wake under <code>wakeRelatch: \"strobe\"</code></h5>\n<p>Set <code>inRows</code> = [0b0100], then <code>reset()</code>. Page 3:7 holds <code>WS</code>, <code>CEND</code>; page 1:0 holds <code>KTA</code>, <code>CEND</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>call</th><th>result</th><th>pc</th><th>halt</th><th>ticks</th><th>Acc</th><th>wake line</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>step 1</td><td>WS</td><td>0xDE0</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>0</td><td><b>1</b> (the strobe write re-latches it: column 0 is out and its key is held)</td></tr><tr><td>step 2</td><td>CEND</td><td>0xDF0</td><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>step 3</td><td>waking: KTA</td><td>0x420</td><td>0</td><td><b>8</b></td><td>4</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>step 4</td><td>CEND</td><td>0x430</td><td>1</td><td>10</td><td>4</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>step 5</td><td>waking: KTA (key still held)</td><td>0x420</td><td>0</td><td>14</td><td>4</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The rival, <code>wakeRelatch: \"external\"</code> (NOT SCORED), on the same program:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>call</th><th>result</th><th>pc</th><th>halt</th><th>ticks</th><th>Acc</th><th>wake line</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>step 1</td><td>WS</td><td>0xDE0</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>0</td><td>0 (the strobe write does not re-latch it)</td></tr><tr><td>step 2</td><td>CEND</td><td>0xDF0</td><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>step 3</td><td>null</td><td>0xDF0</td><td>1</td><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>step 4</td><td>null</td><td>0xDF0</td><td>1</td><td>8</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td><code>updateKLine()</code></td><td>none</td><td>0xDF0</td><td><b>1</b> (no wake yet)</td><td>8</td><td>0</td><td><b>1</b></td></tr><tr><td>step 5</td><td>waking: KTA</td><td>0x420</td><td>0</td><td><b>12</b></td><td>4</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>step 6</td><td>CEND</td><td>0x430</td><td>1</td><td>14</td><td>4</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>step 7</td><td>waking: KTA (key still latched)</td><td>0x420</td><td>0</td><td>18</td><td>4</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<hr>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"gwemu-10-sm510-s15\">15. Where this chapter's facts came from</h4>\n<p>All paths are in the core's source tree as it stood on 2026-10-03.</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><code>src/sm510.mjs</code>:\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>vectors and pin defaults: lines 60–66;</li><li>Io (K flip-flop, <code>updateKLine</code>, <code>commitKLine</code>, <code>rPins</code>): 125–240;</li><li>options and defaults: 254–310;</li><li><code>powerOn</code>: 328;</li><li>wake test and <code>wake()</code>: 348–369;</li><li><code>stepInstruction</code> (halted slot, K clock, tick-before-execute, strobe re-sample): 441–473.</li></ul></li><li><code>src/cpu.mjs</code>:\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>ACL register values (BP = 1): 70–110;</li><li>skip discharge: 190–228;</li><li>unlisted path: 299–308;</li><li>handlers (LB 343, ATPL, RTN, TL, TML, TM/IDX 424, LAX 473, ATBP 503, TIS 551, CEND, IDIV).</li></ul></li><li><code>src/pc.mjs</code>: rule 79–93, <code>DEFAULT_RULE</code> 198, push/pop 274–284.</li><li><code>src/timing.mjs</code>: <code>tick()</code>/gamma wrap 88–103, <code>resetDivider</code> 111, <code>readF</code> 118–126.</li><li><code>src/lcd.mjs</code>: <code>commonEnabled</code> 122–148, <code>plane</code> 152–175.</li><li><code>src/melody.mjs</code>: <code>phaseAt</code>/<code>pinsAt</code> 239–250.</li><li><code>src/ram.mjs</code>: whole file.</li><li><code>src/unestablished.mjs</code>: the 31 <code>CHOICES</code>.</li><li><code>adapter/site-abi.mjs</code>:\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>SM510 construction with <code>wakeRelatch: WAKE_RELATCH</code> (\"strobe\", since 28 Oct 2026);</li><li><code>toLcdArray</code>: 335–368;</li><li><code>step()</code> waking rule: 452–476;</li><li><code>reset()</code>: 478–487;</li><li><code>inB</code>/<code>inBA</code> held: 497–513;</li><li><code>rMuxInvert</code>: 540;</li><li><code>rOut</code>: 598;</li><li><code>updateKLine</code>: 607–617;</li><li><code>_applyWiring</code>: 735–746.</li></ul></li><li><code>spec/isa_sm510.mjs</code>: GROUPS, ALIASES and UNLISTED, 60–300.</li><li><code>reference/READ-sm510-body.md</code>: §0 errata, §2.3–2.5, §3.5, §4.</li><li>The vectors in §14 were produced by running the ABI with scratch scripts <code>sm510_trace.mjs</code>, <code>sm510_trace2.mjs</code>, <code>sm510_k.mjs</code> and <code>sm510_kwake.mjs</code>. They are not part of the tree.</li></ul></section>";
